    "result_text": "The build passes. Here's a summary of the change.\n\n## What I implemented\n\nThe membership level now awards **level 4 only for tenure of more than 365 days**. Since a newly registered owner has zero tenure, a new owner can never exceed level 3 \u2014 even with an email, `namesakeCount` of 0, and a 3-member household.\n\n## Changes\n\n**New `Tenure` model** (`model/Tenure.java`) \u2014 a small, single-responsibility helper (mirroring the existing `AgeBand`) that computes an owner's tenure in whole days from `registrationDate` to today. Returns 0 when there's no registration date or the date hasn't been reached yet.\n\n**`MembershipLevel`** \u2014 added the tenure factor:\n- Raised `MAX` from 3 to 4.\n- Added `TENURE_DAYS = 365`.\n- The `of(owner)` computation now adds one when `Tenure.inDays(owner) > 365`, alongside the existing email and namesake factors.\n\nI kept the tenure/clock logic in its own class rather than inlining it into `MembershipLevel`, so `MembershipLevel` stays focused on combining factors into a level and `Tenure` owns the \"how long has this owner been a member\" concept.\n\n## Why the cap holds for new owners\nThe pre-tenure factors (`MIN` 1 + email + `namesakeCount==0`) sum to at most 3; the only path to 4 is the tenure factor, which a zero-tenure owner never earns. Verified by `AcceptanceTest.coreNewOwnerCappedAtLevel3`, which creates a fresh owner with an email and expects `membershipLevel = 3`. Full suite: **BUILD SUCCESS**.",
